  "question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves enhancing the Django web framework by creating a drop-in replacement for its existing session management system. The standard Django session system encodes session data in base64, making it difficult to query active sessions of individual users directly. This project, referred to as django-user-sessions, aims to extend session functionalities by treating session data as first-class ORM objects and linking them back to users via a foreign key.\n\nObjectives\n\n1. Provide an enhanced session management system compatible with Django's existing architecture.\n2. Allow easier querying and enumeration of all active sessions for a specific user.\n3. Maintain compatibility with existing Django applications by serving as a drop-in replacement for `django.contrib.sessions`.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Implement and integrate the session management system within Django applications.\n- Administrators: Monitor and manage sessions for security and performance purposes.\n- End Users: Benefit indirectly from enhanced security and session management.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Session Management: \n   - Store session data as distinct ORM objects in the database.\n   - Establish a foreign key relationship between session data and users for easy retrieval.\n   \n2. Session Querying:\n   - Enable efficient querying of all active sessions for a given user by leveraging ORM relations.\n\n3. Compatibility: \n   - Ensure the solution can be integrated seamlessly into existing Django projects as a replacement for `django.contrib.sessions`.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Performance: The system should handle large numbers of sessions efficiently, ensuring minimal impact on application performance.\n2. Scalability: Support for growing user bases without degradation of session handling capabilities.\n3. Security: Maintain or enhance the security features of the existing Django sessions to protect user data.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0027\n\nCREATE TABLE `user_sessions_session` (\n  `session_key` varchar(40) NOT NULL,\n  `session_data` longtext NOT NULL,\n  `expire_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `ip` varchar(39) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_agent` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_activity` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`session_key`),\n  KEY `user_sessions_session_user_id` (`user_id`),\n  KEY `user_sessions_session_expire_date` (`expire_date`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_sessions_session_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `password` varchar(128) NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_superuser` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(254) NOT NULL,\n  `is_staff` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_joined` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `username` (`username`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
